    <title>Investigating the Evolvability of Web Page Load Time</title>
    <updated>2018-02-22T14:31:02Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1803.01683v1" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1803.01683v1" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>Client-side Javascript execution environments (browsers) allow anonymous functions and event-based programming concepts such as callbacks. We investigate whether a mutate-and-test approach can be used to optimise web page load time in these environments. First, we characterise a web page load issue in a benchmark web page and derive performance metrics from page load event traces. We parse Javascript source code to an AST and make changes to method calls which appear in a web page load event trace. We present an operator based solely on code deletion and evaluate an existing "community-contributed" performance optimising code transform. By exploring Javascript code changes and exploiting combinations of non-destructive changes, we can optimise page load time by 41% in our benchmark web page.</summary>
